+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/design.md
@@ -0,0 +1,7 @@
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch` checks `Object.hasOwn(operation, "value")`, which is true for an explicit `undefined`.
+
+## Decisions
+
+Treat `operation.value === undefined` as missing, in the same check. No other behaviour changes; the rest of the review found conformance.
diff --git a/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/proposal.md b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/proposal.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..cd9e5e6d9d3fde57575a4608d64aeadf73b4defb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/proposal.md
@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+## Why
+
+A review of `applyPatch` against RFC 6902 found it conforms in every probed case except one: an operation whose `value` member is present but `undefined` is treated as having a value. `undefined` is not a JSON value, so this is equivalent to a missing `value`, which the RFC requires to be rejected.
+
+## What Changes
+
+- `add`, `replace` and `test` operations whose `value` is `undefined` are rejected like a missing `value`.
+- Regression tests pin the edge cases already behaving correctly: move within an array, move onto itself, copy into own child, add at array length, and input untouched on failure.
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+(none)
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: the "Validate the patch" requirement now treats an `undefined` value as missing.
+
+## Impact
+
+`src/index.ts` (`applyPatch` validation) and `src/index.test.ts`. No API change.
diff --git a/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/specs/json-patch/spec.md b/openspec/changes/archive/2026-11-11-reject-undefined-patch-value/specs/json-patch/spec.md
